00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
 In this video, we're going to talk about the constituents of a fiber reinforced polymer,

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,600
 namely the fibers themselves and the resonance used to lay them up.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,560
 We're going to talk about the different kinds of fiber that you might use and how these

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:14,640
 are manufactured and how you select between them. And we'll do the same for resonance as well.

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:19,760
 Picking a particular fiber for use in an FRP lab is often a bit of a balancing act between

6
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:23,840
 the stiffness and the strength of the part that you want to make, but also the density,

7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:28,480
 the weight of that part. And unsurprisingly, different fibers come with different costs as well.

8
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:31,840
 There are endless choices in this space, natural and synthetic fibers,

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:35,920
 fibers that are themselves ceramics or metals and fibers that are polymers.

10
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:40,080
 But probably the three most common fibers for an FRP layup, at least for the kind of engineering

11
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:44,480
 problems you might be looking to solve are fiberglass, Kevlar, and carbon fiber.

12
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,760
 You can of course get all of these materials in a bunch of different grades,

13
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:52,000
 but the most common fiberglass is typically referred to as e-glass,

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,240
 the most common Kevlar Kevlar 49, and the most common carbon fiber

15
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:01,120
 so-called high modulus carbon fiber. Each of these potential fiber constituents have their

16
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:05,760
 own strengths and weaknesses. Fiberglass, for example, being so much cheaper than the other

17
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,480
 alternatives is often used in large structures. And for this reason, you see it very commonly in

18
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:14,960
 boat hulls and in pools, but it doesn't provide the same overall stiffness or strength as

19
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,880
 Kevlar and carbon fiber do. It's worth noting though that there are complexities you can't

20
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:23,600
 strictly pull apart here. Fiberglass, for example, being so much cheaper than Kevlar or carbon

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,120
 you can use way, way more of it to make up the strength or stiffness and usually still spend less

22
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,120
 money. But in circumstances where you're volume limited or you're weight limited and you just can't

23
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:37,920
 throw more and more fiber at the problem like in aerospace components or motorsport components or

24
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:43,280
 in prosthetics, then carbon fiber and Kevlar which are far stiffer and stronger or other things

25
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:47,760
 being equal begin to make a bit more sense. Of these two Kevlar is a little bit stronger and

26
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:52,400
 tougher and is generally known for having better impact resistance. And carbon fiber is a little

27
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:56,320
 bit stiffer. We typically see the former of surfaces that are expected to see a bit of wear and

28
00:01:56,320 --> 00:02:01,440
 we usually see the latter in places where maintaining geometry under load is critically important like

29
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:06,960
 aerodynamic surfaces. And interestingly, many parts are made up as composites of these composites

30
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:12,240
 with carbon fiber in some locations and fiberglass in other locations and Kevlar in other locations

31
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:16,560
 again. Fiberglass is very commonly used to make up the bulk geometry of a part because you can get

32
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:20,880
 a lot of it in there for not very much money. And carbon is often used to reinforce critical

33
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:25,440
 locations like bolt holes. You might also on the same part see Kevlar in locations that you expect

34
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,680
 to see a lot of wear like on the underside of a part near the front of a car that's expected to

35
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:34,080
 maybe scrape the ground every now and again. And alongside making composites of these composites

36
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:39,440
 by mixing and matching distinct regions of carbon fiber and Kevlar and fiberglass, you can actually

37
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:44,880
 get composite fiber mats that are made up of part carbon fiber and part Kevlar for example.

38
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:48,240
 In any case, you need to take a little bit of care if you're going to be mixing and matching

39
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:52,480
 these things. Remember for components that are aligned in parallel for example, each carries a

40
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:56,560
 proportion of the load relative to its stiffness. This means that if you're going to use a stiffer

41
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:01,760
 material to skin a more compliant one like carbon fiber on the outside of fiberglass, you need to

42
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:06,080
 make sure that the thickness of the carbon fiber layer is sufficient that it has the effective

43
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:11,280
 strength required to carry the component of the load that it's going to bear. Because even if

44
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:15,440
 it's going to be far thinner than a fiberglass layer, being so much stiffer it might actually

45
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:20,000
 carry a higher proportion of the load. These fibers of themselves made in a variety of different

46
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,040
 ways. Fiberglass, for example, is made by an extrusion process where molten silica is forced

47
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:30,400
 through a die before eventually being spooled onto a bobbin. And many polymer fibers like nylon

48
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:36,160
 and Kevlar and polyacrylonitrile are precursor to making carbon fiber and made through a process

49
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:41,840
 known as wet spinning. Here, a viscous polymer is condensed and coagulated in a liquid bath and

50
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:46,240
 kind of extracted before being spooled. Carbon fiber is kind of a special case of this where you

51
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:51,200
 then take that polymer fiber and subject it to another process called pyrolysis that essentially

52
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:56,880
 strips off all of the non-carbon elements of the polymer chains and rearranges them in a particular way

53
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:01,360
 to make them stronger and stiffer. That process is quite involved and to describe it in any more

54
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:05,840
 detail would be verging on the boundaries of talking about chemistry. It's probably going to leave it

