00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,960
 there. But I have left some more information about it on the textbook page for this video if you'd

56
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:13,920
 like to check it out. Now, all of these fibers after initial formation and spooling onto a bobbin

57
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:19,360
 are weaved into a fabric into a mat that can be laid up or laminated. As you can imagine, this

58
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:23,440
 process is generally done with industrial sized looms and is highly automated and there are a

59
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:28,080
 bunch of different weave geometries that you can purchase your fibers in. I won't go into too much

60
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,840
 detail on the different types of weave. I've left some interesting resources on the textbook page

61
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:36,720
 if you'd like to check that out that compares between them, but generally selecting between one weave

62
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:41,520
 and another is a function of a few different things. Promoting or mitigating anisotropy in your

63
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:45,920
 part because load will tend to be carried along the direction of the fibers in your weave.

64
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:51,440
 How drapeable the particular weave is how easy it is to form over tight radii when you're laying it up.

65
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:56,240
 The density of fibers in this weave and the subsequent permeation of the polymer when you form

66
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:01,440
 the bulk FRP and the appearance of the final part as well. It's actually quite common. Many

67
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:06,960
 FRPs are made by using variations of different weave geometries or even just variations on the

68
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:11,200
 orientation of a single weave geometry. This is usually done in an effort to try and limit the amount

69
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,520
 of anisotropy in a part by kind of distributing the directions that the layers are oriented.

70
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:19,680
 You can get back to reasonably isotropic behavior, but you should note that even if you could

71
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:24,000
 somehow get totally random alignment of these fibers in the part because you're kind of

72
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,760
 laminating them. You're putting them down one after the other after the other. You're always

